    "result_text": "AcceptanceTest is the only test class, and the full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country national-number length validation for the E.164 telephone on `POST /api/owners`.\n\n**Change** \u2014 `E164Telephone.normalize` (`src/main/java/.../rest/function/owner/E164Telephone.java`) now checks the national-number length against the country code after the existing 8\u201315 digit check:\n- `+61` \u2192 requires 9 national digits\n- `+1` \u2192 requires 10 national digits\n\nA number whose national part is the wrong length for a recognised country code returns `null`, which `NormalizeOwnerTelephone` turns into `InvalidTelephoneException` \u2192 **400** (`{\"errors\":[\"telephone\"]}`) via the existing handler. The country codes are held in a `LinkedHashMap` ordered longest-prefix-first (`\"61\"` before `\"1\"`), and unrecognised country codes fall through unchanged (still bounded by the 8\u201315 digit rule), so no existing behaviour breaks.\n\nI also updated the javadocs in `E164Telephone`, `NormalizeOwnerTelephone`, and `InvalidTelephoneException` to document the new rule.\n\n**Tests**: the full suite is green \u2014 `AcceptanceTest.errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` (posts `+61 123`, expects 400) passes, and `BUILD SUCCESS` overall.\n\nNote: this reuses the existing `NormalizeOwnerTelephone` step rather than adding a new pipeline function, since the length check is intrinsic to producing a valid E.164 number and the `InvalidTelephoneException` \u2192 400 path already covers the rejection.",
